igure></div><p>I have ADHD. I=E2=80=99m super impatient. I want things quic=
kly, and I tend to not do a lot of planning before I start projects - I lik=
e to just dive in. </p><p>AI is like an eager puppy (an expensive one!) - i=
t=E2=80=99ll do whatever you want. You can prompt it to push back, if you t=
hink to, and it won=E2=80=99t do obviously bad things unless you work hard =
to trick it, but mostly, it doesn=E2=80=99t have =E2=80=9Cagency=E2=80=9D o=
r context and just follows instructions. </p><p>That=E2=80=99s kind of bad =
if you have access to powerful tools. It=E2=80=99s very easy to kick off a =
large, long-running project with an Agentic harness, even if it has a poorl=
y defined goal. The AI will happily pursue that goal, and you=E2=80=99re st=
uck with something that=E2=80=99s not complete, workable, or well thought o=
ut. </p><p><span>I=E2=80=99ve found in my own projects that if I take the t=
ime up front to think through the problem, I get much better results. And m=
ore than that, I have to think through, and express, my </span><strong>inte=
nt</strong><span>. If the model understands that clearly, and if I refrain =
from being overly prescriptive on the </span><strong>how</strong><span>, an=
d mostly focus on the </span><strong>what</strong><span>, I get much better=
 results.  </span></p><p>There=E2=80=99s a lot of talk about the =E2=80=9Cd=
eflationary era of tech debt=E2=80=9D, and it=E2=80=99s certainly true that=
 these tools let you retire issues in a cost-effective way that wouldn=E2=
=80=99t have been possible even a few months ago. But failing this =E2=80=
=9Cmodern marshmallow test=E2=80=9D is giving rise to a new and different k=
ind of tech debt: large, complex projects that are functionally and syntact=
ically correct, even well organized (as opposed to what we usually think of=
 as tech debt), but poorly thought out and designed, and as a result, very =
hard to work with and integrate. </p><p>This kind of debt is subtle. The mo=
dels do usually manage to build something workable, or at least sort of wor=
kable. You find yourself chasing endless bugs because somehow, the model ca=
n=E2=80=99t understand what it=E2=80=99s built well enough to debug it, or =
to build it properly. Sometimes the model can=E2=80=99t see enough of the p=
roblem at once to understand it, sometimes the problem just has enough cont=
radictions or under-defined areas to cause problems. </p><p>The best cure f=
or this is patience and using the models themselves to help you take your t=
ime, pressure test the idea, and work out a plan. This goes for non-technic=
al tasks too - often the models will answer questions without really unders=
tanding what=E2=80=99s being asked, and it=E2=80=99s worth taking the time =
to make sure they do, particularly with more complex problems. </p><p>If yo=
u=E2=80=99ve tried to do something with AI and failed, it could be that you=
 haven=E2=80=99t asked the right questions in the right way. Have it ask yo=
u for clarity and have a conversation before trying to solve the problem. <=
